00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.579 align:start position:0%
 
but first though the fight against  coronavirus involves all of us from  politicians to doctors and one of the  few people who straddles both of those  Wells is Labour's shadow Mental Health  Minister Rosanna Alencon who continues  to work as a frontline enemy doctor she  joins us now thank you for being on the  program I know you've been doing some  shifts in the nightingale Hospital in  London what's the situation been like  most recently the staff there are  absolutely incredible and are a  committed team and this week on my shift  I was working with the family support  liaison team and it really gave me the  insight to see how this virus has been  stripping families of the ability to  grieve of humanity and the the setup  that they have at the Nightingale is  phenomenal we spend the day phoning  patients families and I've been a doctor  for many years but there is nothing more  heartbreaking than delivering the very  worst of news over the phone and on some  occasions we are able to bring a family  member through to say goodbye but we're  only allowed to bring one person in and  imagine now for a family having to  decide who is that individual that is  able to go in and say goodbye and  accompanied by a member of the team like  myself that is a crippling decision to  make and I found myself coming home  heartbroken and asking myself how much  of this could have been avoided you say  how much thick could have been avoided  what do you mean by that I understand  that this is a global pandemic it would  have been impossible to save all the  lives that we've lost but we did enter  into lockdown too late we did not  deliver PPE to the frontline when it was  needed we did not follow global advice  on self isolation and we haven't rolled  out mass testing and contact tracing so  when I find myself in the unenviable  position of not being able to hug  someone as they cry after they've said  their last  by it makes me think how many of these  grieving families could have not had to  be in this position and fundamentally we  are going to have real mental health  issues to deal with  for these grieving families but for the  NHS workers who have had to hold their  hand through this process I mean it's a  big statement to make I guess the  government would argue that actually  they are really ramping up testing now  they've been a surprise if you like how  many people have looked have followed  the lockdown and if it had been done  earlier that may not have happened and  also I suppose - it's incredible really  how similar the number of deaths have  been across other European countries  which perhaps doesn't necessarily chime  with the fact that we've done too much  worse and as well I think we should  always compare ourselves to the best not  the average and when you look at other  countries like Germany there have been  far fewer deaths they got on top of this  much sooner with testing and contact  tracing and I firmly believe that yes  the government have made grand promises  of a hundred thousand tests by the end  of April but we have to be clear that  that is delivered on and not simply a  promise we can't be talking about  capacity it has to be tests delivered  but are we too late we are three months  in to having had our first case and  fundamentally it is heartbreaking to see  how many people can't even be at the  funerals of those they love the most  people have been robbed of the  opportunity to say goodbye in a humane  way and NHS workers haven't been able to  comfort grieving families in the way  that they would have been able to  normally but you know I am really proud  of the British public they have been  phenomenal in following advice and I  think the fact that we are seeing cases  come down is testament to the resolve of  our communities but the government also  needs to be recording the numbers of  deaths in care homes and in the  community as well as hospital desk  because we won't know  whether we are fully on top of this and  we're the lockdown measures have been  working as well as they can be until we  are recording all of the deaths as you  say it does feel as if cases have  started to come down according to the  official figures I just wonder if that's  Europe in your experience working in A&amp;E  on the front line I mean they've been  reports as well but the Nightingale  Hospital London has been largely empty  look I've been working at Sir Georges in  Tooting that's where I work in the A&amp;E  in the nightingale obviously it's an  intensive care setup but in the A&amp;E  departments my colleagues and I have  been finding that the number of patients  worryingly  is going down for other illnesses my  plea to anybody watching your show today  Sophie would be if you have chest pain  if your child is ill if you are worried  that someone you love have got symptoms  of a stroke please please go to the  emergency department we are ready and  waiting for you we don't want to see a  rise in deaths come out of those the  people who were too afraid to go to  hospital because they were worried about  contracting kovat 19  yeah there's certainly been some  concerning reports about cancer  treatment being delayed a knee a  tendancy dropping and do you think at  some point there might be a tipping  point where actually there is a risk of  more deaths effectively being caused by  the lockdown than by coronavirus itself  I don't think the deaths will be caused  by the lockdown I think the deaths will  be caused by people being too afraid to  go to emergency departments and having  had their cancer treatments delayed I  think people want transparency our  communities want transparency cancer  patients want transparency people need  to know what is going on when they can  expect their treatment when they might  be able to have that long-awaited  operation but people have to go to the  hospitals if they are concerned that  they are unwell due to something else  that isn't covert 19 related kist Armour  has written to the prime minister a  calling on the government to publish its  exit strategy and now in many ways of  course calling for  something to be published is the easy  bit the hard bit is of course working  out those very difficult finely balanced  decisions but what should happen so what  does labour think the exit strategy  should be labour isn't looking to have  any sort of argument about what the exit  strategy should be we are fully  supportive of the lockdown measures that  are in place what we are asking for is  some strategy going forward some idea  because when we look at our devolved  Nations they have at least given the  public some indication of how the easing  of restrictions may look I think Sophie  so much of the uncertainty is really  breeding people to be worried in our  society they are really really worried  because they don't know is it going to  be children going back to school first  you know when of the elderly going to be  able to leave their homes what is it  going to mean for their jobs so what the  Labour Party is calling for is some  greater understanding and clarity as to  what the exit strategy may look like at  the same time there is calling for  something you know as I said it's the  easy a bit the heart the Hobbit is  coming up with answers and solutions so  labour doesn't have any opinion on that  at all I am sure that Kirsten would be  happy to be invited to have discussions  with the government and draw up a plan  of a positive exit strategy  that works for all our community should  that be them this is about saying people  need to have some understanding of what  the easing of restrictions may look like  if you are someone who relies so heavily  on mental health support for example  because you have an addiction or you  have an eating disorder and you can't go  to your daily therapy programs are you  going to be someone who is able to do  that in the first wave of easing  restrictions if you are a single parent  and you were trying to home-school two  children our children going to be  potentially going back to school first  if you have loved ones that you really  really care for who are in a care home  like my father am I going to be able to  go and see him or where we have to wait  until next year these are so many things  that need answering for people to have  positive mental health going forward  because the British public have shown  incredible resolve they can get through  anything but the government need to make  it slightly easier to ease the pressure  on people's families so that they  understand what the easing restrictions  may look like and that is what the  Labour Party is calling for well we  might get some of those answers sooner  rather than later with Boris Johnson  returning to work from tomorrow from  Monday are you pleased to see the Prime  Minister back of course I wish him well  we all wish him well and we only want  the best for him and his family ok and  just finally and you're in kiss Donna's  Shadow Cabinet you also served under  Jeremy Corbyn and how would the two men  different in their leadership styles  there are two men full of integrity a  love for the Labour Party and a desire  to make our country fair for everyone of  course they have different styles I  think any boss I've ever had in my life  and any job I've had have had different  styles it was an honor to serve our  Labour Party under Jeremy Corbyn and it  is an honor to serve under Kier stahma  so those the similarities but what are  the differences then I think honestly  they have different personality types I  think it was phenomenal to see Kirsten  at PM cues this week he was utterly  forensic in his responses and I think  that was something that was really  welcomed people have have different  styles and I am really excited about the  Labour Party going forward but it was an  honor to serve under Jeremy Corbyn and  I'm not going to sit here and compare  one on the other it was an absolute  pleasure to serve under both okay dr.  Rosanna Alencon thank you very much and  sharing your experiences at the  Nightingale thank you thank you very  much

